WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.760
We ask a lot of questions
about our diet.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.080
What’s the right way to
treat a chronic illness?

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.720
Fight off a virus,
lose weight?

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.640
The problem is, we get
a lot of different answers.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.920
Well, I’m here to help.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.040
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.480
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:23.720
Today I start with my traffic light system
for ranking the relative healthfulness

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:27.640
of Green Light vs. Yellow Light
vs. Red Light foods.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.560
Whenever I'm asked at a lecture
whether some food is healthy or not,

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.040
my reply is: "Compared to what?"

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.520
For example, are eggs healthy?

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:40.160
Compared to some breakfast
sausage next to it? Yes.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.920
But compared to oatmeal?
Not even close.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:47.880
Think of it as having $2,000
in your daily calorie bank.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.160
How do you want to spend it?

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.200
For the same number of calories, you
can eat 1 Big Mac, 50 strawberries,

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.960
or a half a wheelbarrow
full of salad greens.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.400
Now, they don't exactly fill
the same culinary niche;

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.520
I mean if you want a burger,
you want a burger.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.240
And I don't expect
quarts of strawberries

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.360
to make it onto the Dollar Menu

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:10.560
any time soon, but it's an illustration
of how mountainous a nutrition bang

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.200
you can get for the same caloric buck.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.240
Every time we put
somethingin our mouth,

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:19.640
it's a lost opportunity to put something
even healthier in our mouth.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.280
So what are the best foods to eat
and the best foods to avoid?

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.200
Here's how I like to think of it.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.960
This is my Traffic Light system

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.480
to help quickly identify some
of the healthiest options.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.720
Green means go, yellow means caution,

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:39.320
and red means stop... and think
before you put it into your mouth.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.480
Ideally, on a day-to-day basis

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:43.960
green category foods should be maximized,

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:48.520
yellow foods minimized, and
red category foods avoided.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.240
As far as I can figure, the best available
balance of evidence suggests

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.600
the healthiest diet is
one that maximizes

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.600
the intake of fruits, vegetables,

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:57.680
legumes (which are beans, split
peas, chickpeas, and lentils),

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:00.720
whole grains, nuts and seeds,
mushrooms, herbs and spices.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.680
Basically, real food that
grows out of the ground;

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.840
these are our healthiest choices.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.640
In general, the more
whole plant foods

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.600
and the fewer processed
and animal foods, the better.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.760
So more green light foods
and less yellow and red.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:14.840
Like running red lights
in the real world,

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:17.040
you may be able to get away
with it once in a while,

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.640
but I wouldn't recommend
making a habit out of it.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.840
My traffic light model stresses
two important concepts:

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.240
plant foods tend to be healthier
than animal foods

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.240
in terms of being packed
with protective nutrients

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.200
and fewer disease-promoting factors,

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:34.680
and unprocessed foods tend
to be healthier than processed foods.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.520
Is that always true? No.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:40.480
Am I saying that all plant foods are
better than all animal foods? No.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:42.440
In fact, the worst thing
on store shelves has been

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.600
partially hydrogenated
vegetable shortening.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:47.640
It's even got vegetable
right in the name!

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:52.640
Even some unprocessed plants such
as blue-green algae can be toxic.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.480
Anyone's who's ever had
a bad case of poison ivy

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:58.400
knows plants don't always
like to be messed with.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.240
In general though, choose plant
foods over animal foods,

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:04.040
and unprocessed over processed.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.840
What do I mean by processed?

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.560
The classic example is
the milling of grains

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.360
from whole wheat, for
example, to white flour.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:13.280
Isn't it ironic these are then
called "refined" grains,

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:16.360
a word that means improved
or made more elegant?

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:19.520
The elegance was not
felt by the millions

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:22.920
who died from beriberi
in the 19th century,

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.160
a vitamin B deficiency
disease that resulted

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:28.320
from polishing rice
from brown to white.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.720
White rice is now enriched with vitamins
to compensate for the "refinement."

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:34.160
A Nobel Prize was awarded

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.760
for the discovery of the cause
of beriberi and its cure:

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.960
rice bran, the brown part of rice.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.080
Beriberi can cause damage
to the heart muscle,

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.360
resulting in sudden death
from heart failure.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:47.120
Surely such a thing could
never happen in modern times.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.440
I mean an epidemic
of heart disease

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.204
that could be prevented and
cured with a change in diet?

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.600
Sometimes though, processing
can make foods healthier.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.840
For example, tomato appears
to be the one common juice

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:01.160
that may actually be healthier
than the whole fruit.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:03.600
The processing of tomato products
boosts the availability

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:07.200
of the antioxidant red pigment
by as much as five-fold.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:11.920
Similarly, the removal of fat from
cacao beans to make cocoa powder

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:13.520
improves nutritional profile,

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:16.280
since cocoa butter is one of
the rare saturated plant fats

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:20.000
(along with coconut and palm kernel
oils) that may raise cholesterol.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.080
So for the purposes of
the traffic light model,

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:25.760
I like to think of "unprocessed" as

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:29.000
nothing bad added,
nothing good taken away.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.560
So in the above example, tomato
juice could be thought of

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.520
as relatively unprocessed since
even much of the fiber is retained,

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.560
unless salt is added, which
would make it a processed food

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.720
in my book and bump it
out of the green zone.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:44.120
Similarly, I would consider
chocolate processed

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:47.120
since they add sugar, but
cocoa powder not.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:50.280
The limited role I see for yellow
light foods in a healthy diet

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:53.800
is to promote the consumption
of green light foods.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:56.960
They can be the spoonful of sugar
that makes the medicine go down.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.760
So if the only way I can get a patient
to eat oatmeal in the morning

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.520
is to make it creamy with almond milk,
then tell them to add almond milk.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:06.000
The same could be said
for red light foods.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.200
If the only way you're going
to eat a big salad

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:12.200
is to sprinkle it with Bac-Os,
then sprinkle away.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:15.880
Bac-Os are what are referred
to ultra-processed foods,

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:19.280
bearing no redeeming nutritional
qualities or resemblance

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:23.400
to anything that grew out of the ground,
and often with added badness.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.080
Bac-Os, for example, have added
trans fats, salt, sugar, and even

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:31.480
Red #40, a food dye that may cause
thousands of thyroid cancers every year.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:34.400
As a red light food, it
should ideally be avoided,

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:39.080
but if the alternative to your big
spinach salad with Bac-Os is KFC,

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:40.960
then it's better to sprinkle.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:43.800
The same goes for real bacon bits.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:46.600
I realize some people have
religious or ethical objections

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:48.720
to even trivial amounts
of animal products.

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:52.240
Growing up Jewish next to the largest
pig factory west of the Mississippi,

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:54.640
I can relate to both sentiments.

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:58.040
But from a human health standpoint,
when it comes to animal products

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:01.400
and processed foods, it's
the overall diet that matters.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:03.240
For example, without hot sauce

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.640
my intake of dark green leafy
vegetables would plummet.

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:08.920
Yeah, I could try making
my own from scratch,

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:14.400
but for the time being the green
ends justify the red means.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:18.520
On the same note, it's really the
day-to-day stuff that matters most.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:21.360
It really shouldn't matter what
we eat on special occasions.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:25.240
Feel free to put edible bacon-flavored
candles on your birthday cake—

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:27.400
I'm not actually making those up.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:29.960
Though I guess from a
food safety point of view,

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:34.520
a raw cake batter Salmonella infection
could leave you in dire straits.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:38.160
In general, it's really
your regular routine

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:41.000
that determines your
long-term health.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:45.080
Our body has a remarkable ability
to recover from sporadic insults,

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:48.920
as long as we're not habitually
poking it with a fork.

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:51.200
That's why from a
medical standpoint

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:54.160
I don't like the terms
vegetarian and vegan

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:57.240
because they are only defined
by what you don't eat.

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:00.080
When I taught at Cornell,
I had vegan students

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:03.200
who appeared to be living
off of French fries and beer.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:06.640
Vegan, perhaps, but not
terribly health-promoting.

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:11.000
That's why I prefer the term
whole food plant-based nutrition.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:12.720
In general, the dividing
line between

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:15.640
health-promoting and
disease-promoting foods

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:18.760
may be less plant- versus
animal-sourced foods,

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:24.560
and more whole plant foods
versus most everything else.

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:28.840
The Daily Dozen was first
introduced in How Not to Die.

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:32.280
Rather than being a
meal plan or diet in itself,

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:35.400
the Daily Dozen is to
be used as a checklist

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:41.560
to inspire you to eat the healthiest
of healthy foods in your daily diet.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:45.320
What did a pilot study on
How Not to Die’s Daily Dozen

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:49.320
and How Not to Diet’s
21 Tweaks find?

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:51.680
In How Not to Die,
I compiled the healthiest

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:53.640
of the so-called
Green Light foods

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:56.000
into my Daily Dozen checklist
and encouraged people

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:59.200
to try to fit them
into their daily routine.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:01.960
I made it into a free app,
Dr. Greger's Daily Dozen,

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:03.800
available for iPhone
and Android,

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:07.240
so anyone and everyone can try
to check off all the boxes

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:10.320
every day and track
their progress over time.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:13.000
As the feedback poured in
from millions of people

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:14.720
giving the app a try,

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:17.600
two themes of complaints arose.

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.000
The first was
that it was just too much food.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.920
There was no way they could eat
all that food in one day.

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:26.760
In response, I explained
that the Daily Dozen

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:29.000
was aspirational,
something to shoot for,

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:31.560
just a tool to inspire people
to include

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:33.160
some of the healthiest
of healthy foods

00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:34.880
into their daily diets.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.000
The vast volume of food
I prescribed was on purpose.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:40.000
I mean, I was hoping
by encouraging people

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:44.040
to eat so much healthy stuff,
it would naturally crowd out

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:45.760
some of the less-healthy stuff.

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:49.560
After checking off 24 servings
in the Daily Dozen,

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:54.040
there's only so much room left
for a pepperoni pizza.

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:57.560
Ironically, the second
major complaint we got

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:00.480
is that it doesn't have
enough calories.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:02.120
I had to explain
that the Daily Dozen

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:04.520
just represented the minimum
I encourage people to eat,

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:06.760
not the maximum,
and that, certainly,

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:09.520
training athletes requiring
thousands more calories

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:12.080
would have to eat much more.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:13.640
This all got me thinking,
though.

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:17.320
Too much food
but too few calories?

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:20.160
Sounds like
the perfect weight-loss diet!

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:23.480
The Daily Dozen is by definition
all Green Light foods,

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:25.080
all whole plant foods,

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:29.000
so that right there
bakes in all 17 ingredients

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:30.720
of the ideal weight-loss diet

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:34.320
I introduced in my book
How Not to Diet.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:36.200
What about the calorie count?

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:37.560
A systematic review

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:39.920
of successful
weight loss strategies

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:42.160
concluded that given
the metabolic slowing

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:44.920
and increased appetite
that accompanies weight loss,

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:47.640
to achieve
significant weight loss,

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:49.080
calorie counts may need to drop

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:52.880
as low as 1,200 calories a day
for women

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:56.320
and 1,500 calories a day
for men.

00:09:56.320 --> 00:09:57.800
And what do you know?

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:00.040
Plug in common foods
into each of the categories,

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:02.960
and the Daily Dozen averages
about 1,200 calories,

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:04.720
with the higher-calorie
food choices

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:07.600
nailing 1,500 calories.

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:10.000
In the second half
of How Not to Diet,

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:12.160
I focus on things
that can further accelerate

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:14.840
the loss of body fat,
regardless of what you eat,

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:18.200
which I encapsulated
in my 21 tweaks.

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:20.880
My free Dr. Greger's
Daily Dozen app

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:22.680
had become so popular
at that point

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:25.920
that I decided to revamp it
with these new features.

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:29.320
Now, if you switch over
to the weight-loss setting,

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:30.240
you can make a game

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:32.120
out of how many
fat-busting boosters

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:33.520
you can squeeze in every day,

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:36.200
along with
your Daily Dozen checkboxes.

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:39.400
Does this actually help people
lose weight, though?

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:44.360
You don't know
until you put it to the test.

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:47.720
As part of a master's thesis
in lifestyle medicine

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:50.880
at the Lithuanian University
of Health Sciences,

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:52.760
a graduate student conducted:

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:55.240
"Weight control
and lifestyle change

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:58.520
using Dr. Michael Greger's
Daily Dozen and 21 Tweaks:

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:00.440
does it really work?

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:02.360
A pilot study."

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:03.560
How cool is that?

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:05.640
A before-and-after study
with no controls,

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:07.640
so it's really
more like a case series

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:10.680
of four men and four women,
on average obese,

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:12.120
who were encouraged
to use the app

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:15.480
and follow the Daily Dozen
and 21 Tweaks for four weeks,

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:17.120
preceded by a prep phase,

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:18.960
which included
educational sessions

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:20.360
and even a cooking class,

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:23.560
then followed up
for a month after the diet.

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:25.400
At the end
of the four-week intervention,

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:28.000
an average weight loss
of about 15 pounds (6.80 kg)

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:28.880
was achieved,

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:31.680
along with a few inches
dropped from the waist.

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:34.000
The two folks with diabetes
lowered their sugars

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:35.560
by about 50 points.

00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:38.080
LDL cholesterol dropped
by 35 points.

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:39.080
That's the kind of drop
we'd see

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:41.560
with a first-generation
statin drug.

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:45.920
Three reached their ideal range,
under an LDL of 70.

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:47.240
That's really
the most important thing,

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:50.960
as heart disease is
the leading killer in Lithuania,

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:53.080
as it is in the United States.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:54.800
And in addition
to physical health,

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:57.800
mental health appears
to improve as well.

00:11:57.800 --> 00:11:59.560
A month
after the study was over,

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:01.360
the participants appeared
to be able to retain

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:04.040
enough healthy eating habits
to maintain the weight loss

00:12:04.040 --> 00:12:07.600
and improved well-being,
but as they strayed,

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:09.920
their cholesterol
and blood sugars crept back up.

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:12.960
It was so cool to read
all the participants' comments.

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:14.240
They never felt hungry,

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:16.400
their taste buds
started changing,

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:19.400
they even learned
to cook a little oatmeal.

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:21.280
Of course, what they were
really happy about

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:22.440
was losing all that weight

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:25.960
while experiencing
some positive side effects.

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:28.320
Now, obviously
a small, uncontrolled study

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:30.840
is some super weak sauce
evidence,

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:33.960
but what it did show
is that it's possible

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:36.840
to make a lot of diet
and lifestyle changes at once,

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:38.760
and if they're healthy changes,

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:42.616
they may bring
about some healthy effects.

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:45.000
